2.2 Statistical challenges
Biological growth data frequently present characteristics that are not adequately represented by ordinary nonlinear least squares, including:

increasing or decreasing residual variability;
systematic differences in growth parameters among treatments, sexes, populations, or other predictors;
repeated observations from the same experimental unit;
between-unit variation in individual growth parameters;
hierarchical sampling structures;
serial correlation between observations;
incomplete or irregular measurement schedules.

PGM therefore combines Unified growth equations with parameter-specific fixed effects, nonlinear mixed effects, residual variance functions, and residual correlation structures.
These components have distinct interpretations. Fixed effects describe systematic changes in growth parameters, random effects describe persistent variation among grouping units, and residual correlation describes dependence remaining between observation-level deviations.

2.3 Documentation conventions
Throughout this documentation:

\(W(t)\) denotes the fitted growth function;
\(W'(t)\) denotes the instantaneous growth rate;
\(W''(t)\) denotes growth acceleration;
\(t\) denotes time or age;
\(y\) denotes the observed response.

The interpretation of each model-specific parameter is presented in the chapter on growth models.

3.6 Reporting problems
When reporting an error, include:

the PGM version;
the jamovi version;
the operating system;
the selected model and options;
the complete error message;
a reproducible or anonymized dataset, when possible.

4.2 Basic structure
Data should be provided in long format, with one row per observation.
At minimum, an analysis requires:

one numeric response variable;
one numeric time or age variable.

Additional variables may be supplied as:

fixed-effect predictors;
random-effects grouping variables;
residual-correlation grouping variables.

4.6 Missing observations
For each model setup, PGM uses complete observations for:

the selected response;
time;
all selected fixed-effect predictors;
all selected random-effects grouping variables;
all selected Correlation Groups.

Rows containing missing values in any required variable are excluded from that fit.
This can be particularly important when predictors have structurally missing values. After complete-case filtering, a categorical predictor may be left with only one observed level, in which case its effect cannot be estimated.

4.9 Data coverage
A nonlinear growth curve should ideally contain observations from the early, rapidly growing, and late portions of the trajectory.
If the observed data do not adequately cover the upper part of the curve, the asymptotic parameter may be weakly identified and highly uncertain.
Early or truncated datasets may produce:

unstable asymptotes and geometric descriptors;
strong parameter correlations;
wide confidence intervals;
implausible extrapolation;
convergence failures.

When the asymptote is scientifically known or deliberately constrained, PGM allows a positive fixed asymptotic value to be supplied in the Model Setup.
Predictions beyond the observed time range should nevertheless be interpreted cautiously.




