7.4 Ti parameterization
The Ti form uses:

\(A\): upper asymptote;
\(K\): Unified growth-rate parameter;
\(T_i\): time of inflection;
\(d\): Richards shape parameter, when free.

For the three-parameter models, \(d\) is fixed by the selected family.

8.8 Confidence intervals
Approximate 95% confidence intervals are derived from the fitted covariance matrix and the corresponding transformed parameter profile.
For positive transformed parameters, interval limits are returned to the natural parameter scale.
For \(W_0\), PGM uses a log-scale delta approximation for the positive natural-scale quantity.
These intervals are asymptotic approximations and may be unreliable when:

sample size or effective replication is small;
parameters are weakly identified;
the observed trajectory poorly covers the relevant part of the curve;
the fixed-effects design is nearly singular;
the fitted model is numerically unstable.

9.9 Residual error
Residual variation remains at the observation level after accounting for:

the nonlinear mean function;
fixed effects;
random effects;
an optional variance function;
an optional AR(1) or CAR(1) correlation structure.

\(\theta=0\) corresponds to constant variance;
\(\theta&gt;0\) indicates increasing variance with fitted response;
\(\theta&lt;0\) indicates decreasing variance with fitted response.

10.4 Choosing a variance structure
A variance function should be considered when residual spread changes systematically across fitted values or time.
Selection should consider:

residual-versus-fitted plots;
normalized residuals;
information criteria;
parameter stability;
biological plausibility.

A lower AIC alone is insufficient if the fitted variance structure produces unstable parameters or implausible trajectories.




