13.2 Parameter estimates
The Model Parameters table reports growth parameters on their natural interpretation scale.
When no fixed predictors are assigned to a parameter, the table contains its reference estimate.
When fixed effects are present, additional rows represent natural-scale parameter profiles associated with the fitted model coefficients.
For categorical predictors, the first observed level is used as the reference.
For continuous predictors, the reference value is zero.
For interaction coefficients, the displayed parameter profile incorporates the required main-effect and interaction contributions.
Available columns include:

estimate;
standard error;
lower 95% confidence limit;
upper 95% confidence limit;
p-value for fixed-effect coefficient rows.

13.5 Interpreting confidence intervals
Confidence intervals quantify uncertainty under the fitted model and its asymptotic approximation.
Wide intervals may indicate:

insufficient effective replication;
inadequate coverage of the growth trajectory;
strong dependence among model parameters;
weak identification;
excessive model complexity;
numerical instability.

14.5 Comparing models
AIC, AICc, and BIC comparisons require:

the same response variable;
the same response scale;
the same observations;
compatible likelihood definitions;
the same treatment of missing values.

RMSE, MAE, and related error metrics should also be compared only across the same observations.
Model selection should combine statistical performance, residual diagnostics, parameter plausibility, convergence, and interpretability.

nonconstant residual variance;
systematic curvature;
regions of poor fit;
influential or extreme observations.

14.2 Residuals versus time
This plot is used to investigate:

temporal trends not represented by the growth function;
early- or late-stage model inadequacy;
changes in residual variability over time;
local clusters of positive or negative residuals.

17.2 Recommended information
An academic report should identify:

the PGM version;
the jamovi version;
the selected Unified growth equation;
the Ti or W0 parameterization;
whether the asymptote was estimated or fixed;
the response and time units;
the number of observations;
the fixed-effects structure for each growth parameter;
relevant interaction terms;
the random-effects structure and grouping hierarchy;
the residual variance structure;
the residual correlation structure and Correlation Groups;
the estimation framework;
the model-comparison criteria;
the residual diagnostics examined.

